    {
      "type": "LinguisticObject",
      "content": "Baroness Olga de Meyer, by 1905 [lent to London 1905]. Annie Swan Coburn (Mrs. Lewis Larned, 1856–1932), Chicago, by 1932; bequeathed to her friend Walter Stanton Brewster (1872–1954), Chicago, 1932 [<a href=\"https://publications.artic.edu/sites/default/files/file_assets/AIC1931CoburnWill_comb_OCR.pdf\">Last Will and Testament and Letters Testamentary of Annie S. Coburn, Dated August 9, 1932</a>, Article 3 (p), as <em>Girl’s Head</em>]; given to the Art Institute of Chicago, 1933.",
      "classified_as": [
        {
          "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435438",
          "type": "Type",
          "_label": "Provenance Statement",
          "classified_as": [
            {
              "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300418049",
              "type": "Type",
              "_label": "Brief Text"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "LinguisticObject",
      "content": "In addition to large-scale portraits, James McNeill Whistler created small-scale likenesses, often on wood panel, as in this painting of a young sitter. A well-known beauty, Olga Caracciolo lived in Dieppe, France, likely where this work was executed, and later married the photographer Adolf de Meyer. Rather than a swift, abbreviated study for a larger composition, Whistler considered a work such as this to be a satisfying aesthetic whole.",
      "classified_as": [
        {
          "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026032",
          "type": "Type",
          "_label": "Summary",
          "classified_as": [
            {
              "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300418049",
              "type": "Type",
              "_label": "Brief Text"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "LinguisticObject",
      "content": "<p>In addition to large-scale portraits, James McNeill Whistler created small-scale likenesses, often on wood panel, as in this painting of a young sitter. A well-known beauty, Olga Caracciolo lived in Dieppe, France, likely where this work was executed, and later married the photographer Adolf de Meyer. Rather than a swift, abbreviated study for a larger composition, Whistler considered a work such as this to be a satisfying aesthetic whole. Indeed, small paintings serve as invitations for an intimate viewing experience and contemplation of the artist’s harmonies of color and form.</p>\n",
      "classified_as": [
        {
          "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416",
          "type": "Type",
          "_label": "Description",
          "classified_as": [
            {
              "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300418049",
              "type": "Type",
              "_label": "Brief Text"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "format": "text/html"
    }
